  "success": true,
  "output": "## Performance Analysis — Canon\n\nScope note up front: `engine/` (Python) is entirely unimplemented scaffolding — every pipeline function (`blocking.py`, `detect.py`, `normalize.py`, `graph.py`, `db.py`, `main.py`) is a `raise NotImplementedError` stub per the Phase 1–3 build order in AGENTS.md. There's no engine code to audit yet, so everything below is `web/`.\n\n### 1. N+1 query patterns — none found\n\nThe data layer is already deliberately engineered against this. `lib/server/conflicts.ts::getQueuePage` batches rows/counts/total via `Promise.all`, then does one grouped `countChildrenByParent` query for the whole page instead of per-row (comment literally says \"Three round trips, not three hundred\"). `lib/server/export.ts` and `lib/server/audit.ts` follow the same pattern. Good baseline — nothing to fix here.\n\n### 2. Unnecessary React re-renders — real issue in `ConflictQueue`\n\n`components/conflicts/conflict-queue.tsx` holds `selectedIndex` at the top of the tree and re-renders on every `j`/`k` keystroke. In the ledger view it maps rows like this:\n\n```tsx\n// conflict-queue.tsx (current)\n{rows.map((row, index) => (\n  <ConflictDiffRow\n    key={row.conflictId}\n    row={row}\n    selected={index === clamped}\n    onSelect={() => setSelectedIndex(index)}       // new closure every render\n    submit={submitFor(row.conflictId)}              // new closure every render\n    onOverrideOpenChange={(open) => setOverrideOpen(row.conflictId, open)} // new closure\n    ...\n  />\n))}\n```\n\nNone of `ConflictDiffRow`, `ListRow` (conflict-focus.tsx), or the dense-table row button are wrapped in `React.memo`, and even if they were, three props get a fresh function identity on every parent render. Result: selecting a different row in a 400-conflict queue (AGENTS.md's own target scale) re-renders every row — re-running `diffSegments` character-diffing, `conflictFieldLabel`, `severityReason`, etc. for rows whose visible state didn't change.\n\nFix — stabilize identity per row, then memo the leaf:\n\n```tsx\n// conflict-diff-row.tsx\nexport const ConflictDiffRow = React.memo(function ConflictDiffRow({ ... }) { ... });\n```\n\n```tsx\n// conflict-queue.tsx — bind once per row via a stable callback keyed by id/index\nconst handleSelect = useCallback((index: number) => setSelectedIndex(index), []);\nconst handleOverrideOpenChange = useCallback(\n  (conflictId: string, open: boolean) => setOverrideOpen(conflictId, open),\n  [setOverrideOpen],\n);\n\n{rows.map((row, index) => (\n  <ConflictDiffRow\n    key={row.conflictId}\n    row={row}\n    selected={index === clamped}\n    index={index}\n    onSelect={handleSelect}              // stable reference, pass index in\n    conflictId={row.conflictId}\n    onOverrideOpenChange={handleOverrideOpenChange}\n    submit={submit}                       // stable useReview().submit + conflictId as separate prop\n    ...\n  />\n))}\n```\nThen inside `ConflictDiffRow`, call `onSelect(index)` / `submit(conflictId, action)` / `onOverrideOpenChange(conflictId, open)` — the closures now live inside the memoized component, not in the parent's `.map()`, so `React.memo`'s shallow-prop comparison actually bails out for the ~398 rows whose props didn't change. Apply the same pattern to `ListRow` in `conflict-focus.tsx` and the row `<button>` in `conflict-dense-table.tsx`.\n\n### 3. Caching opportunities\n\n`sources` and `rulesets` are read on almost every console page (`/runs/new`, `/rules`, and indirectly via `loadQueue`'s `getRuleset` lookup), but both are low-write / high-read tables — rulesets are explicitly immutable once versioned. Every one of these calls hits Postgres fresh, and `connection()` at the top of each function opts the whole page out of any static caching:\n\n```ts\n// lib/server/sources.ts — wrap with unstable_cache, tag-invalidate on write\nimport { unstable_cache } from \"next/cache\";\n\nexport const listSources = unstable_cache(\n  async (): Promise<Source[]> => {\n    const rows = await getDb().select().from(sources).orderBy(sources.createdAt);\n    return rows.map((row) => sourceSchema.parse(row));\n  },\n  [\"sources-list\"],\n  { tags: [\"sources\"] },\n);\n```\n```ts\n// app/api/sources/route.ts\nimport { revalidateTag } from \"next/cache\";\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  ...\n  const source = await createSource(parsed.data);\n  revalidateTag(\"sources\");\n  return Response.json({ source }, { status: 201 });\n}\n```\n\n`getRuleset(id)` in `lib/server/rulesets.ts` is the strongest candidate: a ruleset row is immutable by design after creation (AGENTS.md: \"never edited in place\"), so it can be cached **with no revalidation at all**, keyed by id — `loadQueue` calls it on every queue page load to resolve `appliedRuleStrategy` labels, and that lookup will never go stale for a given id.\n\n### 4. Memory leaks — none found\n\nChecked every `useEffect`/`setInterval`/`addEventListener` in `web/components/`: `run-live.tsx`'s elapsed-time ticker clears its interval on unmount/`running` change, `conflict-queue.tsx`'s `keydown` listener is removed in its cleanup, `conflict-focus.tsx`'s `scrollIntoView` effect has no subscription to leak. No `ResizeObserver`/`IntersectionObserver`/WebSocket usage anywhere. The Postgres client in `lib/db/index.ts` is correctly singleton-cached on `globalThis` so HMR reloads in dev don't open a new pooler connection per reload. Nothing to fix here.\n\n### 5. Redundant computations\n\n`components/rules/ruleset-editor.tsx` recomputes the evaluation order and then does `O(n)` lookups inside the render loop:\n\n```tsx\n// current — O(n²) for n rules\nconst ordered = orderRulesByEvaluation(rules);   // sorts every render, no memo\n{ordered.map((rule) => {\n  const index = rules.indexOf(rule);             // O(n) per row\n  return <RuleRow key={rule.id} position={ordered.indexOf(rule) + 1} ... /> // another O(n)\n})}\n```\nWith a handful of rules this is invisible, but it's needless: track index in the sort itself instead of re-deriving it.\n\n```tsx\nconst ordered = useMemo(\n  () => rules.map((rule, index) => ({ rule, index }))\n             .sort((a, b) => ruleSpecificity(b.rule) - ruleSpecificity(a.rule) || a.index - b.index),\n  [rules],\n);\n{ordered.map(({ rule, index }, position) => (\n  <RuleRow key={rule.id} rule={rule} position={position + 1} onChange={(patch) => update(index, patch)} ... />\n))}\n```\n\nThe other redundant-computation source is the same one as §2: because rows aren't memoized, `diffSegments()` (the character-level diff in `lib/diff-chars.ts`) reruns for every value in every row on every selection change, even though it's a pure function of `(value, other)` that hasn't changed for 398 of 400 rows. Fixing the memoization in §2 eliminates this for free — no separate change needed.\n\n---\n\n**Priority order**: #2 (re-renders) is the only finding with real user-facing impact once the conflict queue is wired to a route — it directly undermines the \"keyboard-only pass over 400 conflicts\" design goal stated in AGENTS.md. #3 (caching) is a cheap, safe win. #5 is a nice-to-have at current scale.\n",
